# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files. Never committed: .env.local carries a Blob token for the client document store
# and .env.production.local carries production's database password and the Vercel bypass
# secret.
.env*
# ...except the template, which is the whole point of having one, and which had never been
# committed at all — so `cp .env.example .env.local` in the README failed in every clone.
# It holds no values.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts
.storage


# Generated by e2e-import-agent.mjs — large synthetic PDFs, rebuilt on every run.
.e2e-tmp

# Written by checks/home.mjs before it archives the working list, so an interrupted run can be
# recovered by the next one. Absent when nothing is parked.
.parked-projects.json

# Throwaway probe scripts written beside the suites during debugging.
.[a-z].mjs

# WinQS reference reports — the format this platform's printed bill was modelled on.
#
# The folder is gone: the conventions it was kept for are in `src/lib/report/bill-pdf.ts` now — the
# ruled column grid, "Not Priced" stated on the row, the rand mark in its own column and the double
# rule under a carried figure. Nothing in the build ever read the PDFs themselves.
#
# The rule stays so that dropping a fresh set in to compare against cannot commit them by accident.
# One correction to what this note used to say: they were **not** without extractable text — it
# claimed that as the reason they could not be vouched for, and every one of them carried between
# four and forty-four thousand characters.
winqs_reports/

# Captured output from the long-running scripts, redirected so a run that outlives a terminal can
# still be read: `npm run check > check.out`, and the same for the deploy and the seeder.
#
# `deploy.mjs` is the reason these must be ignored rather than merely untidy. It labels a
# deployment with the commit it is of and marks the label "(+ uncommitted changes)" when the tree
# is dirty — and `> deploy.out` creates that file *before* the script reads the tree, so every
# deploy captured this way accused itself of shipping uncommitted work. Verified: the first
# labelled preview carried the suffix against a tree that was clean.
check.out
deploy.out
seed.out

# Tesseract language data, cached on first OCR
*.traineddata

# Client and personal documents dropped into the repo root while working.
#
# Nothing here is source. They arrive because the repo root is a convenient place to drag a file
# to, and they are exactly what must never be committed: a contractor's priced bill is client
# data, and `F7487239_S214076784_DSR27158100.pdf` turned out to be somebody's graduation letter,
# carrying their full name and student number. All five were sitting untracked while `git add -A`
# ran twice in the same session; they escaped by minutes.
#
# Ignored rather than deleted, because they are the user's files and this is their working copy.
/*.pdf
/*.xlsx
/*.docx
